      "final_numeric_score": 2.7,
      "narrative_summary": "This proposal addresses an important mesoscale question—how 3D genome organization and chromatin state give rise to emergent, cell-type-specific transcriptional programs in the human brain—and offers a creative multimodal AI strategy (ViT/transformer embeddings, contrastive alignment to scGPT, and downstream prediction/interpretation). If successful, the resulting embeddings and models (scCOE/scGEP) could be impactful community resources and enable new integrative analyses of brain cell regulation and neurodegeneration.\n\nThe main weaknesses are feasibility and rigor tied to data realities: the plan depends heavily on scarce or absent human brain co-assay datasets, alignment of modalities measured in different cells, and transfer learning assumptions that may not hold across tissues/species/disease states. The proposal would be strengthened by explicitly enumerating target public datasets (with accessions), defining harmonization and batch-correction strategies, specifying evaluation metrics and negative controls (including cell-type confounding tests and ablations), and providing a concrete open-science/reproducibility plan (public pipelines, containers, model cards, and release schedule)."
